    "description": "# Refactored legacy radar processing algorithms for integration into certifiable commercial flight software, improving maintainability and enabling compliance with timing constraints.\n\n# Designed low-latency C data structures optimized for real-time performance, meeting deterministic execution and strict resource constraints required by DO-178C.\n\n# Performed in-depth timing and performance profiling of radar processing pipelines to identify optimization opportunities critical to system-level certification and throughput targets.\n\n# Collaborated cross-functionally with FPGA/hardware teams to ensure tight integration and synchronization between software and hardware signal processing.\n\n# Designed and implemented a custom software rendering pipeline for 3D terrain, replacing OpenGL to reduce dependency on safety-certified graphics drivers and streamline certification under DO-178C guidelines.\n\n# Previously integrated OpenGL-based terrain visualization, leveraging sensor-derived data while adhering to graphics safety standards for embedded avionics displays.\n\n# Built and maintained internal debug and test tools to support radar and graphics subsystems, improving development efficiency, traceability, and certification readiness.\n\n# Developed a hybrid Modular Open Systems Approach (MOSA) integration framework supporting both Future Airborne Capability Environment (FACE) and Open Mission Systems (OMS) standards, enhancing modularity and platform interoperability.\n\n# Engineered and implemented Real-Time Operating System (RTOS) software applications, adhering to ARINC653 standard for optimal performance and reliability.\n\n# Proficiently integrated third-party Flight Management Software into mission systems utilizing the cutting-edge FACE Modular Open Systems Approach (MOSA).\n\n# Utilized Model-based Systems Engineering (MBSE) methodologies using Cameo modeling tool, facilitating collaborative design and development processes.",
